gan by coordinating all the help  that was needed in our neighborhood such  as shopping for vulnerable elderly  people going to the pharmacy or walking  their pets  one day a company contacted us and said  that they had a batch of dairy products  that they couldn't use they asked if we  wanted them and we said yes bring them  over  they did  and then we distributed the milk and  yogurt and so on  Julia came to somos tribu every morning  to help out before going to work as a  lab technician in the Gregorio marignon  hospital at the height of the pandemic  the maranion was overflowing with more  than 1100 coveted patients with hundreds  more turning up every day  what impressed me was the silence  hospital is normally allowed and lively  place always bustling with activity but  during the pandemic it was as quiet as a  cemetery and there was a kind of horror  we'd never experienced before many  people were deeply affected by what  happened then and still are  I worked in the emergency unit  we didn't have enough face masks when  the pandemic hit  there just wasn't enough material for  them  material  another founding member of Somos tribu  is Adriana pop she decided to act after  hearing of shortages of face masks and  protective clothing for health and  essential workers  I've always loved sewing so I found some  tutorials on how to make face masks  I used my daughter's bed sheets to make  them  the first person who asked for a mask  was a bus driver  that's when I started in earnest  I set up a WhatsApp group of women who  knew how to sew we got in touch with  health workers who didn't have masks and  gowns and got to work  we made these masks for fruit Sellers  and grocery store workers  these were nice for young people we made  these ones with cartoon characters for  children a lot of kids had a hard time  understanding what was going on  Adriana wasn't scared even though she  delivered masks to people working in  essential services  became sick  people would suffer  cousins so I didn't think about myself  one of the most important AIDS provided  was the distribution of food to the  elderly struggling with lockdown and  families unable to go out because they  had come down with covet 19. again  coordinated all the food that was  packaged and sent out from palomeras  the few of us who had cars brought food  to people's homes here's where we set up  a food bank  over there was a Fruit Stand and over  there was rice lentils things like that  at first we prepared more than 80 food  baskets a day for families that's a lot  whole apartment buildings had come down  with covid we left the food at the main  entrance and the residents handed it out  we couldn't go inside elderly people's  homes so we left the food at their front  door  then we called them to say we brought  the food our only contact with people  was by phone  these are the Flyers we first made with  phone numbers on them  we put them in our cars so that the  police wouldn't fine us for breaking  lockdown  the Highlight for somos tribu was  receiving the European Citizen Award  from the European Parliament  Brussels gave us the award you can read  about it online  last year in March members of the  European Parliament came to a park  nearby to give us their recognition  and in November we went to Brussels to  collect our award  no longer exists its Legacy lives on in  other groups that serve smaller  neighborhoods in the vaishas district of  Madrid the center at palomeras is a  resource that supports vulnerable women  but they still distribute food to the  elderly and help people who are  suffering the impact of long covert  [Music]  that's it for today see you next week on  the covid-19 special till then stay  healthy  [Music]  [Music]
 

